timestamp=2026-11-04T09:49:44.499Z level=INFO run=2e1ba946 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python << 'PY'\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.stats import chi2\n\n# \"The critical value for the chi-square distribution should be set to 0.01.\"\n# Sometimes in some texts they mean the significance level alpha is 0.01; in others they just give the critical value number. \n# But 0.01 is tiny as a critical value for df>=1. Or maybe they mean the test statistic threshold is 0.01 in some transformed way? No. \n# Another possibility: for Mahalanobis, compare d^2 to chi2_{df, 1-alpha}; if they say \"critical value ... set to 0.01\", maybe alpha=0.01 so threshold is chi2.ppf(1-0.01, df). That matches common usage. But why say \"set to 0.01\" referring to critical value? Or maybe the question as written: \"The critical value for the chi-square distribution should be set to 0.01.\" as a parameter/setting - perhaps the threshold value is exactly 0.01? That gives us ~2369 outliers - very aggressive. Let us look at the full task again more carefully.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
