timestamp=2026-11-04T09:53:43.921Z level=INFO run=2e1ba946 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python << 'PY'\n# Let us think - do technical noise count as \"outliers\" in the final list? Yes, they are values that \"could not possibly occur in reality\" and we discarded them; they are essentially outliers/invalid observations. Also the final question says \"Return list of all outliers indexes.\" - this is the most comprehensive set.\n# Also check for possible overlaps and make sure we didn't double-count. The total seems plausible given 560 technical noise + others.\n# But let us also look at the Mahalanobis outliers - Mahalanobis was computed on df_nostd (after removing standard 3*IQR outliers). So Mahalanobis outliers are a different set of observations in that reduced space. Are these Mahalanobis outliers considered \"outliers\" to be included in the final answer list? The instruction says \"Identify outliers...\" using Mahalanobis and \"consider observations for which squared distance exceeds...\" as outliers. So yes, they are outliers to be reported.\n# Also \"Remove standard outliers from the data...\" (3*IQR) - standard outliers are removed. But the question asks for \"Return list of all outliers indexes.\" So all outliers identified throughout the process - including those removed. That matches returning their indices.\n# Also categorical outliers - \"If so they should be also considered as outliers.\" So include them.\n# This combination gives us the full set.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
